swi_a#show ip mroute
(10.38.22.40, 232.5.5.5), uptime 00:00:22 ... Outgoing interface list: vlan48 (1)

  => STEP 4 PASS, with the two readings distinguished. On the multicast ROUTER's
     interface the authored expectation is met exactly: both tables update the instant
     the interface goes down and PIM drops it from its active set, and both state and
     dataplane recover on the way back up. On the host's L2 access port the tables
     correctly do NOT update, for a topological reason, and the soft state times out
     instead. The recovery leg also shows that SSM source filtering is still enforced
     after an interface flap: 9 of 10 for the joined channel and 0 of 10 for the wrong
     source in the same leg.

STEP 5 -- removal of the SSM range (designed), and finding F-14                    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the negation is asymmetric, so the syntax was read off the device first:

awplus(config)#no ip pim ssm default
% Invalid input detected at '^' marker.
awplus(config)#no ip pim ssm ?            <-- read-only probe, tools/qmark.py, no CR
  <cr>
                                      <-- the positive form takes `default|range <acl>`
                                          and the negation takes NEITHER: bare
                                          `no ip pim ssm` (recorded as O-27)

  With `no ip pim ssm` applied on BOTH routers and `ip pim ssm default` verifiably gone:

awplus#show running-config | include pim
service pim
 ip pim sparse-mode
 ip pim sparse-mode                    <-- no `ip pim ssm` line anywhere

  the existing channel kept forwarding 10 of 10 and its (S,G) survived. That is NOT a
  defect: an IGMPv3 (S,G) include join is legitimate in plain PIM-SM too and joins the
  shortest-path tree directly, so removing the SSM range need not tear it down. It does
  mean the dataplane cannot show the removal on 232/8, so removal was proved by an
  admission test instead -- and that attempt is what produced F-14:

  (with NO SSM range configured at all)
  v2 (*,G) join on 232.7.7.7      -> swi_a#show ip igmp groups : NOT listed. Still rejected.
  (then `access-list 20 permit 239.20.20.20 0.0.0.0` + `ip pim ssm range 20` on BOTH)
  v2 (*,G) join on 239.20.20.20   -> 239.20.20.20  vlan48  00:00:07  00:04:14  0.0.0.0
                                     ACCEPTED, inside the configured SSM range
  v2 (*,G) join on 232.7.7.7      -> still NOT listed, 40 s later, same table read:

swi_a#show ip igmp groups
Group Address    Interface            Uptime   Expires  Last Reporter
239.20.20.20     vlan48               00:01:07 00:03:14 0.0.0.0        <-- configured range
(232.7.7.7 absent)                                                     <-- IANA range

  The impact was then measured rather than assumed: traffic from an arbitrary source to
  239.20.20.20 was 0 of 10 in the dataplane (cap s10-ssmrange-starg-eth4.pcap, 10 of 10
  instrument control), because no RP is configured, so the accepted membership is inert
  and nothing leaks. The ACL and the range were removed and `ip pim ssm default`
  restored.

  => STEP 5 PASS as a measurement, and it produced F-14: the IGMPv2 any-source-join
     rejection is bound to the HARDCODED IANA 232/8 range only, and is NOT applied to a
     range configured with `ip pim ssm range <acl>`. Measured on one router, one
     interface, two groups, in a single `show ip igmp groups`; and the rejection on
     232/8 persists with no SSM configuration at all, so `ip pim ssm default` is not
     what enforces it. RFC 4607 section 4.2 expects a router to apply SSM semantics to
     its configured range too. It is a spec-conformance gap that makes `ip pim ssm
     range` weaker than `ip pim ssm default`, not a forwarding hole -- the accepted
     membership forwards nothing. Worth a developer's eye.

STEP 6 -- persistence across a reboot, re-measured in the dataplane (designed)     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: nothing is written to startup-config. The configuration is staged in a NAMED
  boot config, the DUT is reloaded from it, and the boot pointer is put back afterwards.
  swi_a was NOT reloaded or power-cycled.

awplus#copy running-config flash:/ck43865.cfg  -> Successful operation
awplus(config)#boot config-file flash:/ck43865.cfg
  tools/tb ckreload tb277 ckt1406c u2 115200 480
    -> 'reboot system? (y/n):' y;  login after 61 s

awplus#show boot -> Current boot config: flash:/ck43865.cfg (file exists)
awplus#show running-config | include ssm
ip pim ssm default
awplus#show spanning-tree brief
  port1.0.48 Designated Discarding       <-- the known 17-21 s post-reload window
awplus#show spanning-tree brief          (re-read)
  port1.0.48 Designated Forwarding       <-- only now is it safe to measure

  Then a channel never used before the reload, with its wrong-source control in the same
  leg:

cap s11-postreload-eth4.pcap: 18 frame(s)
  x10 ICMP t8/c0 ... (10/10 instrument control)
  x8  UDP 10.38.22.40 -> 232.6.6.6 :5001 [ours] [eth 88:9d:98:df:26:02 -> 01:00:5e:06:06:06]

  The boot pointer was restored to flash:/default.cfg and flash:/ck43865.cfg deleted,
  its `(y/n)[n]` prompt answered with tools/ckyn.py.

  => STEP 6 PASS: 8 of 10 for (10.38.22.40, 232.6.6.6) and 0 of 10 for (10.38.22.44,
     232.6.6.6) -- a channel and its wrong-source twin, both first used after the
     reload, in the same leg. The configuration survived, the dataplane works, and SSM's
     source filtering is still enforced after a cold boot, which is the half a config
     read cannot show.
